program multiple times is how this  approach helps students appreciate how  any kind of analysis can be structured  in a very logical way  after they understand the logic we then  introduce a variety of different types  of projects where they can see the  application of rna sequencing techniques  in oncology in precision medicine  in infectious diseases and epidemiology  with the popularity of single cell  we've seen a uh kind of like an  expectation that this is just exactly  the same as bulk rna sequencing and it  really is not there are a lot of new  methods there are a lot of specific  approaches and a lot of emphasis is put  on downstream interpretation and so as  we have heard today  now we have a full section that kind of  automates and simplifies the downstream  analysis of single cell rnac data using  such packages like silverat  and monocle and importantly it we  provide a step-by-step breakdown of each  one of these logical steps  using exercises that you can perform  independently and i'm excited to say  today that this course is also available  and anyone can find that course on the  onwix logic portal  obviously supplementing the basic  training with project examples is a big  uh  part of what we are trying to do and so  we take these uh publications that have  an impact  in this domain and we break down the  methodology proportion and really  describe how one can analyze such data  and replicate an analysis found in a  publication  with so many different types of omics  data now it's important to not only  understand how to process analyze and  visualize a single type of data but  importantly integrate multinomics data  as well and that's a big part of what we  do on this portal is introduce  multi-omics integration methods that  could be contrasted and compared and  applied in specific domains with a  benchmark so an important part of what  we do in the projects are curated data  sets that one can use to establish an  understanding and really test how well  you apply the methods to get the same  results  as we've seen over the past two years uh  infectious diseases from uh being kind  of a domain that suffered from a lack of  funding  became a domain with exciting new  research and applications that touch the  lives of all of us today and so back in  2000 back in 2020 we launched our  bioinformatics for infectious  diseases program  and we quickly adapted that program to  address the challenges around covet 19  so that not only we're able to  understand pathogens and kind of study  those pathogens using project examples  but also think about applications  and i'll briefly kind of mention a  couple of these applications that we've  seen  resulting from  this program  so as a result of understanding the  methodology and seeing how next  generation sequencing can be used to  study these viruses and and different  types of infectious diseases  we have developed a number of  collaborations around wastewater  sequencing of covet 19 uh monitoring uh  variants of concern and and other types  of uh kind of strange phenotypes that  have been reported in literature  and i think that um you know this kind  of interest in bioinformatics as a  result of the pandemic  of course the pandemic in itself was an  unfortunate event that we're still not  out of but i think it really gave a lot  of popularity  to  terminology methods and even types of  data  that will in the future i think this  generation that went through this uh  pandemic  will uh be so aware and so familiar with  some of the methodology that they  will be able to address uh future  challenges of similar character in a  much more effective way  and so uh we've designed several project  examples a variety of different viral  epidemics and an in-depth training  resource for sars cove 2  where both consensus level and  sub-consensus level analysis could be  performed  not only at the sequence level but  importantly also translating the  sequence level to the immunology and to  the protein protein interaction level so  looking at 3d structures of proteins and  understanding how  they have an effect on vaccine design  and protein protein interaction  and so really at the core of this i  again want to emphasize how significant  it is  that this is available not only for  engineers  uh the t-bone for platform really hides  the complexity of analytical pipelines  and complex algorithms that have been  developed by experts in the field  to make it possible for someone who's a  student  or a biologist or a biochemist to apply  these complex methods and understand how  they are used with practical examples  so methods like processing fasta and  fast q files for multiple sequence of  alignment and comparative analysis of  different sequences  reporting  generating a full report on variants of  concern found in different types of  viral populations  understanding how this data can be  visualized effectively and presented  and also importantly as i mentioned  mapping the mutations that we identify  and understanding how they impact  different aspects of immunology  during this these exercises that we  actually also  look at protein protein interaction  really trying to understand uh the  available resources uh like protein uh  crystallography but also how to connect  the different types of data  and really understand uh the the way  that multiple sources of data could be  used together  now yesterday we spoke with uh dr  sonavasutivan from georgetown university  medical school where she leads a program  on systems medicine and together with  her team and her students we've  developed this program specifically to  introduce a non-engineer to the basics  of data science and machine learning  using r and python  i think that it's a very critical skill  for anyone to learn how to code and i  think that it's  non-trivial for someone who doesn't come  from an engineering background to do so  but  we've seen  how these resources can make it much  easier for someone to learn how to code  quickly and develop a practical sense of  the application of languages like r and  python to address bioinformatics  challenges and so the exercises that we  have available on the the portal include  mapping mutations for metabolism of  different drugs uh really learning how  to code from a syntax perspective from  the perspective of data visualization  uh and the application to many different  types of data such as genomic data  analysis metagenomic data analysis as  well as uh different types of big data  repositories that could be explored and  visualized using such tools  to  you know summarize i think that you know  what we have seen is an explosion of uh  interest in  different domains uh starting from you  know very basic usage of genomics in the  lab  to  such interesting concepts like  spaceonics and uh when i mentioned that  we're opening this platform to other  contributors this is really what uh i am  implying  in different universities in different  labs and in different research fields  experts have dedicated  years if not decades of their life to  identifying important topics and  learning how to  address them in a reproducible and high  quality way  and we  would like to kind of establish a way  for those experts to be able to take  their expertise  and  allow us to design high quality training  materials that could help effectively  many of the people that are  locked out of these domains  to be able to participate by first  learning about these topics and then  knowing how to apply their skills and  their insights and innovation and maybe  creative thinking to address the topics  that are  pressing and urgent in each one of these  domains  so we have these programs  on cancer biology precision medicine  spaceomics and and many others and we're  excited to  share with you that you know these types  of programs have already impacted  hundreds of students in louisiana  thousands of students around the world  and we're hoping that many more across  the national association of idea  principal investigator network as we are  completing our first pilot which um uh  lasted from january until the end of  march so until yesterday  so with that um i am going to be  available to answer any questions uh if  anyone had  any questions  i'm going to share my email address if  you would like to reach out to me  i'd love to talk to you about these  programs i've shared my email in the  chat  we will have a few more speakers from  nepy  speaking about  different efforts that we are going to  kind of combine into this program as  well  and uh i thoroughly enjoyed uh seeing  some of the results that  we have seen and i think that is really  the demonstration of what we are trying  to do some of the research projects that  we've seen from the students  so thank you again  definitely okay thank you very much of  course well that was again a great talk  by dr truble
 

